   "source": [
    "# Geant4 Particle-Detector Reconstruction with Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "**Saqib Umair**  \n",
    "MSc Particle and Nuclear Physics, University of Edinburgh\n",
    "\n",
    "## Aim\n",
    "\n",
    "This project uses detector-response data from a layered Geant4 simulation to reconstruct particle type and incident energy. The analysis compares conventional cut-based/calibration methods with machine-learning models across five particle species: photons, electrons, charged pions, neutrons and muons.\n",
    "\n",
    "The detector response contains five silicon-tracker layers, five ECAL layers, five HCAL layers and a muon-gas response. Four beam energies are studied: 100 MeV, 1 GeV, 4 GeV and 10 GeV.\n",
    "\n",
    "![Simulated detector geometry](../figures/detector_geometry.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Main results\n",
    "\n",
    "- Conventional full-particle PID accuracy: **64.5%**\n",
    "- Random Forest PID accuracy: **95.4%**\n",
    "- Conventional energy reconstruction: **R² = -0.570**, RMSE = **4.85 GeV**\n",
    "- Linear regression: **R² = 0.722**, RMSE = **2.04 GeV**\n",
    "- Random Forest regression: **R² = 0.939**, RMSE = **0.957 GeV**\n"
   ]
  },
