   "source": [
    "# Step 1 Detector Geometry and Design\n",
    "\n",
    "The detector was built in Geant4 as a simple layered particle detector with four main subsystems:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. a **silicon tracker**\n",
    "2. an **electromagnetic calorimeter (ECAL)**\n",
    "3. a **hadronic calorimeter (HCAL)**\n",
    "4. a **muon chamber**\n",
    "\n",
    "The aim of this design is to distinguish different types of particles by how they interact with matter and where they deposit energy in the detector.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Overall detector concept\n",
    "\n",
    "The detector is arranged along the beam direction so that particles first pass through the tracker, then the ECAL, then the HCAL, and finally the muon chamber. This follows the standard logic of high-energy particle detectors:\n",
    "\n",
    "- charged particles can be detected early in the tracker,\n",
    "- electromagnetic particles mainly shower in the ECAL,\n",
    "- hadrons penetrate more deeply and shower in the HCAL,\n",
    "- muons pass through the calorimeters and can be identified in the muon chamber.\n",
    "\n",
    "This layered design allows both **particle identification** and **energy reconstruction**.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Silicon tracker\n",
    "\n",
    "The first part of the detector is a **silicon tracker**. Silicon is sensitive to charged particles because they lose energy by ionisation as they pass through the material.\n",
    "\n",
    "This part of the detector helps distinguish:\n",
    "- **electrons**, which are charged and therefore leave tracker signals,\n",
    "- **photons**, which are neutral and do not directly ionise before converting,\n",
    "- other charged particles such as pions and muons.\n",
    "\n",
    "The tracker is therefore especially useful for separating **gamma** and **electron** events.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Electromagnetic calorimeter (ECAL)\n",
    "\n",
    "The electromagnetic calorimeter is built from **5 sampling layers** made of alternating:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **lead**\n",
    "- **liquid argon**\n",
    "\n",
    "Lead is used as the **absorber material** because it has high atomic number and high density, so it efficiently initiates electromagnetic showers through processes such as:\n",
    "- bremsstrahlung for electrons,\n",
    "- pair production for photons.\n",
    "The density of lead is approximately 11.34 g/cm³ (or 11,340 kg/m³) at room temperature.\n",
    "\n",
    "Liquid argon is used as the **active material** because it records the energy deposited by the charged particles produced inside the shower. \n",
    "I tried the PbWO₄ (lead tungstate) and Bismuth Germanate (or BGO) but the lead and liquid argon give me better results \n",
    "\n",
    "In this detector, the ECAL is designed to measure **electromagnetic showers**, so it is expected to contain most of the visible energy from **electrons** and **photons**.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Hadronic calorimeter (HCAL)\n",
    "\n",
    "The hadronic calorimeter is also built as a **5-layer sampling calorimeter**, with alternating:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **iron**\n",
    "- **liquid argon**\n",
    "\n",
    "Iron is used as the absorber because hadrons require a dense material to undergo repeated strong interactions and develop a hadronic shower. Liquid argon again acts as the active material that records deposited energy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The HCAL is intended to measure particles such as:\n",
    "- **charged pions**\n",
    "- **neutrons**\n",
    "\n",
    "Compared with the ECAL, hadronic showers usually develop more deeply and fluctuate more strongly. For this reason, the HCAL is important for distinguishing hadrons from electromagnetic particles.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "An additional **iron slab** is placed after the calorimeters as an absorber, helping to stop strongly interacting particles before the muon system.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Muon chamber\n",
    "\n",
    "The final subsystem is a **muon chamber**, modelled as an aluminium cylinder containing a gas mixture.\n",
    "\n",
    "The chamber gas is defined as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **93% argon**\n",
    "- **7% CO₂**\n",
    "\n",
    "with density:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **1.8 mg/cm³**\n",
    "\n",
    "Argon is commonly used in gaseous detectors because ionising particles can create electron-ion pairs in the gas. CO₂ is added as a quencher to improve detector stability and reduce unwanted discharge effects.\n",
    "\n",
    "The muon chamber is designed to identify **muons**, which are highly penetrating particles. Unlike electrons and hadrons, muons usually do not shower strongly in the calorimeters and can travel through the detector to the final layer. If a particle leaves relatively small calorimeter energy but still produces a signal in the muon chamber, this is characteristic of a muon-like event.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Detector operation\n",
    "\n",
    "The detector works by exploiting the fact that different particles interact differently with matter:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **photons** and **electrons** produce electromagnetic showers, so they mainly deposit energy in the ECAL,\n",
    "- **pions** and **neutrons** produce hadronic showers, so they deposit more energy in the HCAL,\n",
    "- **muons** lose relatively little energy in the calorimeters and can reach the muon chamber,\n",
    "- **charged particles** also leave signals in the silicon tracker, while neutral particles generally do not unless they first interact.\n",
    "\n",
    "By recording the energy deposited in each detector region, the detector can be used to reconstruct both:\n",
    "- the **particle type**\n",
    "- the **particle energy**"
   ]
  },
