    "# 2. Defining the functions\n",
    "\n",
    "## Derived detector features\n",
    "\n",
    "In addition to the raw layer-by-layer energy deposits, several derived features were constructed to summarise how energy is distributed across the detector. These features are physically motivated because different particle species leave different patterns of energy deposition.\n",
    "\n",
    "### ECAL fraction\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\text{ECAL\\_frac} = \\frac{\\text{ECAL\\_sum}}{\\text{E\\_detected}}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the fraction of the total detected energy deposited in the electromagnetic calorimeter.  \n",
    "It is useful because **electrons** and **photons** are expected to deposit a large fraction of their visible energy in the ECAL through electromagnetic showering.\n",
    "\n",
    "### HCAL fraction\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\text{HCAL\\_frac} = \\frac{\\text{HCAL\\_sum}}{\\text{E\\_detected}}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the fraction of the total detected energy deposited in the hadronic calorimeter.  \n",
    "It helps identify particles such as **pions** and **neutrons**, which tend to penetrate beyond the ECAL and develop hadronic showers in the HCAL.\n",
    "\n",
    "### HCAL-to-ECAL ratio\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\text{HCAL\\_over\\_ECAL} = \\frac{\\text{HCAL\\_sum}}{\\text{ECAL\\_sum}}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "This compares the amount of energy deposited in the HCAL relative to the ECAL.  \n",
    "A small value indicates an **electromagnetic-like event**, while a larger value suggests a **hadronic-like event**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### ECAL front fraction\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\text{ECAL\\_front\\_frac} = \\frac{\\text{EM1} + \\text{EM2}}{\\text{ECAL\\_sum}}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "This measures how much of the ECAL energy is deposited in the first two ECAL layers.  \n",
    "It is a simple **shower-shape feature**. Electromagnetic showers often start early, so this quantity can help distinguish electrons and photons from particles that shower later or more irregularly.\n",
    "\n",
    "### HCAL back fraction\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\text{HCAL\\_back\\_frac} = \\frac{\\text{HCAL4} + \\text{HCAL5}}{\\text{HCAL\\_sum}}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "This measures how much of the HCAL energy is deposited in the last two HCAL layers.  \n",
    "It is another shower-shape feature and gives information about how deeply the shower penetrates into the hadronic calorimeter. Larger values can indicate deeper hadronic activity.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Numerical stability\n",
    "\n",
    "A very small number \\(\\varepsilon\\) is added in the denominators when computing these quantities. This prevents division-by-zero errors in events where the detected energy in a subsystem is extremely small."
   ]
