    "\n",
    "### Why the calibration constants are different\n",
    "\n",
    "The calibration constants vary strongly between particle types because different particles interact with the detector through different physical processes.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Gamma and electron** events have calibration constants close to 1.17–1.23. This is because both particles produce electromagnetic showers, and the detector measures these reasonably well in the ECAL. Their visible response is relatively stable and predictable.\n",
    "- **Pions** have larger calibration constants, around 1.45–2.11. This indicates that the detector records only part of the true hadronic energy, so a larger correction is needed.\n",
    "- **Neutrons** have even larger and more irregular calibration constants, reaching values above 6 for some energies. This reflects the fact that neutrons are neutral hadrons and their detector response is highly fluctuating and less efficiently measured.\n",
    "- **Muons** behave differently. At low and moderate energies their constants are near 1, but at high energy the calibration constant becomes much larger. This happens because muons are penetrating particles and deposit only a limited fraction of their beam energy in the detector. As the beam energy increases, the deposited energy does not scale in the same way as for showering particles, so the correction factor becomes much larger.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why gamma and electron are reconstructed best\n",
    "\n",
    "The gamma and electron results are the strongest in this section. Their biases are very small and their resolutions improve with energy.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is physically expected because both particles produce **electromagnetic showers**, which are relatively compact and regular compared with hadronic showers. Most of their visible energy is deposited in the ECAL, and the Pb–LAr sampling structure is well suited to measuring that shower. In addition:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **electrons** are charged, so they also leave tracker activity through ionisation\n",
    "- **photons** are neutral, but after conversion they also generate electromagnetic secondaries in the ECAL\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the detector is effectively designed for electromagnetic measurements, the response to gamma and electron is comparatively stable. That is why the calibration constants are consistent and the reconstructed energy has small bias and good resolution.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why pion reconstruction is worse\n",
    "\n",
    "Charged pions interact hadronically, not electromagnetically. Hadronic showers are much less regular than electromagnetic showers because:\n",
    "\n",
    "- strong interactions produce a more complex shower structure\n",
    "- part of the energy can go into invisible channels such as nuclear breakup\n",
    "- event-to-event fluctuations are larger\n",
    "- the shower can extend more deeply into the detector\n",
    "\n",
    "As a result, the visible energy from pions is not measured as cleanly as for gamma or electron events. This explains the larger biases and poorer resolutions seen for pions, especially at low energy.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why neutrons are particularly difficult\n",
    "\n",
    "Neutrons are the most difficult hadronic particles in this detector. The main reasons are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- neutrons are **neutral**, so they do not leave direct tracker ionisation\n",
    "- their hadronic interactions are highly irregular\n",
    "- the visible fraction of their energy fluctuates strongly\n",
    "- some of their energy can remain poorly measured in the detector\n",
    "\n",
    "This is why the neutron calibration constants are large and unstable, and why the neutron resolutions are poor. For example, the neutron bias is very large at 100 MeV, 1000 MeV, and especially 10000 MeV. This means that even with file-by-file calibration, neutron energy reconstruction is still unreliable compared with the electromagnetic case.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why muons behave differently\n",
    "\n",
    "Muons are not showering particles in the same way as electrons, photons, or hadrons. Instead, they mainly lose energy through ionisation and tend to behave like penetrating minimum-ionising particles.\n",
    "\n",
    "This means that:\n",
    "\n",
    "- the calorimeters do not measure the full beam energy of the muon\n",
    "- the deposited energy is only weakly related to the incoming beam energy\n",
    "- the muon chamber is more useful for identification than for precise energy reconstruction\n",
    "\n",
    "This explains why the calibration constants for muons become large at high energy. For example, at 10 GeV the detector only records a relatively small visible energy, so the calibration factor must be large to recover the beam energy. The reconstruction is still acceptable in bias for some muon samples, but the physical meaning is weaker than for electromagnetic particles because the deposited energy is not a direct calorimetric measurement of the full muon energy.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why the PID efficiency is still limited\n",
    "\n",
    "Even though the per-file calibration improves the energy reconstruction, the particle-identification efficiency remains only about 64%.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is because calibration and PID are different tasks. The calibration constants are applied using truth-separated files, but the conventional PID itself still relies on simple threshold cuts. Those cuts are not flexible enough to handle all five particle types well, especially at low energy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The efficiency is worst at 100 MeV and 1000 MeV because:\n",
    "- the total visible signal is smaller\n",
    "- fluctuations are larger in relative terms\n",
    "- the detector signatures of different particles are less clean\n",
    "\n",
    "The efficiency improves at 4000 MeV and 10000 MeV because the detector signals become larger and the shower structure is easier to distinguish.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Physical conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "The per-file calibration shows that the detector response is strongly **particle dependent**. A separate calibration constant works well for the electromagnetic case and improves the numerical reconstruction for many particle types, but it also makes clear that different particles do not share a universal detector response.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main physical lessons are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **electromagnetic particles** are reconstructed best because the detector is well matched to ECAL-dominated showers\n",
    "- **pions and neutrons** are harder because hadronic showers fluctuate strongly and are less completely measured\n",
    "- **muons** are fundamentally different because their deposited energy is not a strong proxy for the full beam energy\n",
    "- **low-energy events** are harder across the board because the detector signals are smaller and more fluctuation-dominated\n",
    "\n",
    "This section therefore demonstrates that the detector can measure different particle types, but also shows the limitations of a purely conventional reconstruction. That is the motivation for the later machine-learning improvement stage."
   ]
  },
