   "source": [
    "### Conventional full-particle reconstruction results\n",
    "\n",
    "The conventional full-particle reconstruction performs poorly overall.\n",
    "\n",
    "For particle identification, the conventional cut-based method gives an accuracy of **64.5%** on the test set. This means that only about two thirds of the events are classified correctly. The performance is uneven across particle types.\n",
    "\n",
    "The best recall is obtained for **electrons** at **0.96**, which means most true electrons are found. However, the electron precision is only **0.52**, showing that many non-electron events are being incorrectly labelled as electrons. This means the conventional cuts are too loose in some regions of feature space.\n",
    "\n",
    "The weakest class is **pi-**, with an F1-score of **0.49**. This shows that pions are the hardest particle type to identify using simple threshold cuts. This is physically reasonable because hadronic showers fluctuate strongly and charged pions can produce detector signatures that overlap with both muons and electromagnetic-like events.\n",
    "\n",
    "Neutrons and muons also show limited performance. Muons have high precision (**0.96**) but only moderate recall (**0.50**), which means that when the detector identifies a muon it is usually correct, but it misses many true muons. This is again consistent with the detector physics, since pions and muons can both behave as penetrating charged particles and are not easily separated by simple cuts.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the conventional classification report shows that the detector contains useful particle-identification information, but the full-particle problem is too complex to be handled well by a small number of fixed selection thresholds.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Conventional full-particle energy reconstruction\n",
    "\n",
    "The conventional energy reconstruction is even weaker. The mean absolute error is about **3014 MeV**, so on average the reconstructed energy differs from the true energy by roughly **3 GeV**. The root mean squared error is even larger, about **4853 MeV**, showing that some events are reconstructed very badly and that large errors occur frequently.\n",
    "\n",
    "The most important result is the coefficient of determination:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R^2 = -0.57\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "A negative \"R\" Squared means that this reconstruction performs worse than a trivial model that would simply predict the mean energy for every event. In other words, the conventional full-particle calibration is not just imperfect, but fundamentally a poor description of the detector response.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Physics interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "This poor performance is physically expected because a single conventional calibration and simple cut-based PID are too crude for a detector that measures very different particle types:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **gamma** and **electron** mainly produce electromagnetic showers in the ECAL\n",
    "- **pions** and **neutrons** produce hadronic showers, which fluctuate more strongly\n",
    "- **muons** are penetrating particles and do not deposit their full beam energy in the calorimeters\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the detector response is strongly particle dependent, one universal conventional reconstruction method cannot describe all classes well. This is why the conventional full-particle baseline performs poorly and why a more flexible method, such as machine learning, is needed."
   ]
  },
